## 5. UX / visual encoding expectations _(semantic — colors come from design.md, not from here)_

- **The build-up is the hero. Read it bottom-to-top.** The four-tier vertical stack must read clearly as a build-up — Tier 1 (atoms) should sit at the visual foundation with the most chips and the densest grid; each tier above should be visibly smaller in chip-count but larger in chip-prominence, with Tier 4 (the Variation Cycle loop) as the visual culmination at the top of the build-up. The eye should walk up the stack the same way a reader walks up the article's "building the program" section. Use vertical white space between tiers and thin horizontal divider rules with tier headers to make the layers unmistakable.
- **The connectors between tiers carry the lesson.** The thin lines from compound chips (Tier 2) down to their constituent joint movements (Tier 1) are not decoration — they are the proof that compounds *emerge from* joint movements. Draw two of them boldly (Squat and Vertical Pull as named examples) and the rest at lower opacity so the diagram doesn't become a tangle. Same for the carryover arrows running up the side of the build-up: they should read as quiet but unbroken.
- **The Variation Cycle loop is the second-loudest element after the headline.** It's the article's most novel idea (most readers have heard of joint movements and compound patterns; few have heard of lifecycle-driven rotation as a programming system). Give it visual weight: a clean cycle shape, distinct stage labels, the iteration spiral exiting clearly. The loop should read as a *piece of engineering*, not a generic process flowchart — sharp corners, mono labels, no organic curves.
- **The left rail is a spec sheet, not a list of cards.** Density is the point. Each rule is one row; the `CODE · LABEL · → why` format must repeat exactly so the rail reads as instrument-bay rows, not eleven separate components. Visual unity in the rail is what makes it scan-able as a single "operating procedure" zone rather than eleven competing cards.
- **The failure-modes strip is the third semantic-color element.** Use the same engineering-instrument convention from Part 3.1's fault annex — a clearly different surface treatment (bordered, slightly recessed, monospace headers) so the strip reads as a separate *instrument warning bar*, not a continuation of the build-up. Apply the design system's **warning** semantic role to the fault chips only — reserved here for the failure-modes strip. Nothing else in the poster gets the warning color.
- **Semantic color, rationed across the whole poster (three states max):**
  - **Success** role — apply *only* to the four compound chips that are completely covered when both pillars are in place (i.e., a subtle success accent on the Variation Cycle loop perimeter to mark "long-term progression mechanism active"); ration tight.
  - **Warning** role — *only* on the five failure-mode chips at the bottom.
  - **Danger** role — *not used in this poster.* The danger collapse already lives on the Part 3.1 curve; using it here would dilute the meaning. Keep the assembly diagram in neutral chart-ink — this is a *build sheet*, not a warning panel.
- **The seven axes rail is ordinal but not ranked.** The article lists the seven axes as peer dimensions, not a hierarchy. Render them at equal visual weight, in the article's order, with a small offset for Safety to mark it as the non-stimulus consideration. The axes rail's job is to be a *lookup* during rotation decisions, not a hierarchy to memorize.
- **Hierarchy of attention:** headline outranks everything → the build-up assembly (with the Variation Cycle loop at its top) is the hero zone → the standing-orders rail is the second-loudest *zone* (but each row inside it is quiet — density is its loudness) → the failure-modes strip is tertiary → the manual header chip and footer are quiet chrome.
- **Restraint = the mood.** This poster carries more information than Part 3.1, so visual restraint is even more important. Dark console ground, mono labels, neutral chart ink for the build-up, semantic color rationed to the cycle perimeter and failure-mode chips only. If everything is loud, the four-tier story collapses.
- **Saveability:** treat it as a reference card the lifter can pin up beside the rack and consult every few sessions. Use chip border weight, header treatment, and tier-divider rules — not hue — to carry the lesson when printed in grayscale or screenshotted on a small phone. The build-up's tier order must survive grayscale.

## 6. Style Reference — **LOCKED for the entire Fit series**

**Fit pillar style: "Engineering Diagnostic Instrument."** Every poster in the Fit series (Aesthetic & Strength, Athletic, Performance Enhancement) uses this same visual language so the pillar reads as one body of work:

- Schematic, technical, measured — fault charts, gauges, operating-band curves, performance envelopes, matrices, signal traces, lookup tables, process strips, dashboards, **assembly schematics, exploded-view build-ups, reference cards with operating spec sheets.** The lifter's body framed as a *system you instrument, read, and assemble*. (This poster's sub-archetype within that language: a **build-up assembly schematic with a standing-orders rail and failure-modes strip** — engineering reference card / system schematic / shop manual exploded view.)
- Mono technical labels and tick-marks (design.md's JetBrains Mono role), display face reserved for headline + key terms (design.md's Inter display).
- Status-indicator color logic (semantic states), ruler-drawn lines, generous negative space, dark console ground (all via design.md).
- Reference points: engine/aircraft fault charts, lab instrument panels, Renaissance Periodization's whiteboard volume-landmark curves rendered as proper engineering chrome, **shop-manual assembly diagrams, system schematics with operating-procedure call-outs, IKEA-but-rendered-as-aerospace exploded views**, *Information is Beautiful* clarity, Wait-But-Why explanation with an engineering accent.

**Policy:** do not change style mid-pillar. Keep this style for all remaining Fit articles; choose a *new* locked style only when the series moves to a different pillar (e.g., Productive, Healthy). Logged in `Series Style Policy — If You're So Smart, Why Are You Not.md`.

## 7. What to Avoid

1. **The hand-drawn-coach whiteboard / "bro-trainer" aesthetic.** The obvious draw is to render this as a chalkboard with arrows scribbled between body diagrams — split routines, "Push Day / Pull Day" panels, dumbbells in the corners, the works. That betrays both the article and the pillar style. The article's intellectual move is to *replace* the body-part / split-routine vocabulary with the joint-movement → compound-pattern vocabulary, and the poster has to embody that replacement in its visual language. No body silhouettes. No arrows on a chalkboard. This is a *shop manual*, not a workout poster.
2. **A simple 11-item rule list as the whole poster.** It is tempting to drop the assembly entirely and just render the four growth rules + seven execution rules at full size, like a wall poster of commandments. That loses the article's bigger half — the building-blocks architecture and the Variation Cycle — and reduces a manual to a slogan list. The standing-orders rail is *one* zone, not the whole card. The article is "rules **AND** building the program" — both nouns; both visible.
3. **A pyramid / Maslow-style stack as the build-up shape.** The temptation is to draw the four tiers (atoms → molecules → variations → cycle) as a pyramid because "atoms at the bottom = wide base." That implies a *hierarchy* (the lower tier is more fundamental) which is correct, *but it also implies a single best-vs-worst gradient*, which is wrong here — the Variation Cycle at the top isn't "better than" joint movements; it's a different layer of the system. Use a flat rectangular build-up with clearly-labeled tier divider rules instead. The shape should read as *layers of a system schematic*, not as *Maslow's hierarchy of training*.
4. **Coloring each compound pattern its own color.** The temptation is to give Squat, Hinge, Push, Pull, etc. each a distinct hue so the connector lines from Tier 2 down to Tier 1 are color-coded. This would saturate the build-up with hue, destroy the rationed-semantic-color policy from §5, and make the failure-modes strip's warning color blend in instead of stand out. Keep all six compound chips in the same neutral instrument ink; differentiate them by *label* and *connector line*, not by hue.
